IMPORT prelude

-- Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1944 (NSW) -- Part 2 (s 2, survival of causes of action after death), the
-- whole operative Act, from the seat of the claimant.
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a claim where someone has died: either the executor or administrator (or the family member
-- acting for the estate) of a person who died with a claim, or a person whose claim is against someone who has died.
-- Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Does the claim survive the death?", "Can the estate recover
-- this kind of damages?", "Is my claim treated as existing against them although they died before I suffered the
-- damage?", "Does the estate's claim cut down the family's own claim?". The court gets no seat.
--
-- Provisions: s 2(1) (survival, with the proviso's exceptions); s 2(2) (the damages the estate recovers); s 2(4)
-- (damage suffered at or after the wrongdoer's death); s 2(5) (Compensation to Relatives Act 1897); s 2(6) (the motor
-- accidents Acts); s 2(7) (s 12B of the Dust Diseases Tribunal Act 1989, asked as facts). Out: s 1 (name), s 3 (a
-- transferred savings provision of a 1984 amending Act, whose text is not deposited): machinery. Ss 2(3) and 4 are
-- repealed.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a flat "shall not apply",
-- "shall not include", "not in derogation", a "where" condition that fails), or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on
-- the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. The damages question answers a named outcome.
--
-- The Act has no duty with a deadline (the time limits for a surviving claim are in the Limitation Act 1969), so no
-- regulative rule is written.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; corpus document dated 7 December 2007; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING.
-- Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1944`

§§ `The nouns`

-- Whose death the question is about. s 2(1): causes of action "subsisting against or vested in the person shall survive
-- against, or, as the case may be, for the benefit of, the person's estate".
DECLARE `Whose death` IS ONE OF
    `the person who had the claim, whose estate I act for`
    `the person my claim is against`

-- The kind of cause of action. The first four are the s 2(1) proviso's exceptions; breach of promise to marry is limited
-- by s 2(2)(b); a Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 claim is named in s 2(5). Each kind is a borrowed term, asked.
DECLARE `Kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `defamation`
    `seduction`
    `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other`
    `a claim under Division 2 of Part 3 of the Property (Relationships) Act 1984`
    `breach of promise to marry`
    `a claim by relatives under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`
    `any other cause of action`

-- The kinds of damages s 2(2) speaks of, as the estate would list them.
DECLARE `Kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `exemplary damages`
    `loss of earning capacity or earnings for the years after death the person would have lived but for the wrong`
    `loss of earnings or earning capacity up to the death`
    `medical, care and other expenses incurred before the death`
    `pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, or curtailment of expectation of life`
    `funeral expenses`
    `another loss to the estate because of the death`
    `a gain to the estate because of the death, such as insurance paid on death`
    `damage to the estate flowing from a breach of promise to marry`

-- The answer to "can the estate recover this kind of damages?" (s 2(2)).
DECLARE `What section 2(2) means for these damages` IS ONE OF
    `left out: the estate's damages do not include it, or are worked out without it`
    `not cut down by section 2(2): the estate recovers it if the cause of action gives it`
    `funeral expenses may be included`
    `the claim does not survive for the estate, so there are no estate damages under this Act`
    `this Act does not settle it`

-- The other Acts ss 2(5) and 2(6) name.
DECLARE `Other scheme` IS ONE OF
    `the dependants' claim under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`
    `the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance) Act 1942`
    `the Transport Accidents Compensation Act 1987`
    `the Motor Accidents Act 1988`
    `another Act`

§§ `s 2(1) proviso -- the causes of action that do not survive`

-- "Provided that this subsection shall not apply to causes of action for defamation or seduction or for inducing one
-- spouse to leave or remain apart from the other or to claims under Division 2 of Part 3 of the Property (Relationships)
-- Act 1984."
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is excepted by the proviso to s 2(1)` `the kind` IF
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `defamation` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `seduction` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a claim under Division 2 of Part 3 of the Property (Relationships) Act 1984` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 2(4) -- damage suffered at or after the death of the person liable`

-- s 2(4) "Where damage has been suffered by reason of any act or omission in respect of which a cause of action would
-- have subsisted against any person if that person had not died before or at the same time as the damage was suffered,
-- there shall be deemed, for the purposes of this Part, to have been subsisting against the person before the person's
-- death such cause of action ... as would have subsisted if the person had died after the damage was suffered."
-- A "where" condition: when it fails, JUST FALSE ("this subsection deems nothing on these facts").
@export Is my claim treated as having existed against the person who caused my damage before they died, although they died before or at the same time as I suffered the damage (s 2(4))?
GIVEN `they died before or at the same time as I suffered the damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person your claim is against died before, or at the same moment as, you suffered the damage (for example, a driver killed in the crash that injured you)
      `I would have had a claim against them if they had been alive when I suffered it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, apart from their death, their act or omission would have given you a cause of action against them for that damage
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my claim deemed to have existed against them before they died` MEANS
    IF     `they died before or at the same time as I suffered the damage`
       AND `I would have had a claim against them if they had been alive when I suffered it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 2(1), (4), (5) -- survival of the cause of action`

-- s 2(1) "Subject to the provisions of this section, on the death of any person after the commencement of this Act all
-- causes of action subsisting against or vested in the person shall survive against, or, as the case may be, for the
-- benefit of, the person's estate". s 2(5) "... so much of this Part as relates to causes of action against the estates
-- of deceased persons shall apply in relation to causes of action under the said Act [Compensation to Relatives Act
-- 1897] ... as it applies in relation to other causes of action not expressly excepted from the operation of
-- subsection (1)."
-- Order: the proviso is a flat negative (JUST FALSE) and is taken first. A death before the Act commenced, and a cause of
-- action that did not subsist at the death (and is not deemed to by s 2(4)), are outside the subsection's scope, which
-- has no "only": NOTHING (forks F1, F2). The commencement date (1944) is not in the deposited text, so whether the death
-- came after it is asked.
@export Does the claim survive the death, for the estate of the person who had it, or against the estate of the person it is against (s 2(1), (4), (5))?
GIVEN `whose death` IS A `Whose death` @desc Who has died. One of: "the person who had the claim, whose estate I act for", "the person my claim is against"
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc The kind of cause of action. One of: "defamation", "seduction", "inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other", "a claim under Division 2 of Part 3 of the Property (Relationships) Act 1984", "breach of promise to marry", "a claim by relatives under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897", "any other cause of action" (for example negligence causing injury)
      `the death was after the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death happened after this Act commenced (in 1944); answer yes for any death since then
      `the cause of action existed when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the moment of the death, the cause of action already existed: held by the person who died, or against them
      `they died before or at the same time as I suffered the damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only where the person your claim is against has died: whether they died before, or at the same moment as, you suffered the damage (answer no otherwise)
      `I would have had a claim against them if they had been alive when I suffered it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only where the person your claim is against has died: whether, apart from their death, their act or omission would have given you a cause of action for that damage (answer no otherwise)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the claim survive the death` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `is excepted by the proviso to s 2(1)` `the kind of claim` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `the death was after the Act commenced`) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit without "only": a death before commencement is outside s 2(1); fork F1)
           IF `the cause of action subsisted` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (scope limit without "only": no cause of action subsisted at the death, so there is nothing for s 2(1) to carry over; fork F2)
    WHERE
        `the cause of action subsisted` MEANS
               `the cause of action existed when they died`
            OR (    `whose death` EQUALS `the person my claim is against`
                AND `Is my claim deemed to have existed against them before they died` `they died before or at the same time as I suffered the damage` `I would have had a claim against them if they had been alive when I suffered it` EQUALS JUST TRUE)

§§ `s 2(2), (7) -- the damages the estate recovers`

-- s 2(2) "Where a cause of action survives as aforesaid for the benefit of the estate of a deceased person, the damages
-- recoverable for the benefit of the estate of that person:
--   (a) shall not include: (i) any exemplary damages, or (ii) any damages for the loss of the capacity of the person to
--       earn, or for the loss of future probable earnings of the person, during such time after the person's death as
--       the person would have survived but for the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of action,
--   (b) in the case of a breach of promise to marry shall be limited to such damage, if any, to the estate of that
--       person as flows from the breach of promise to marry,
--   (c) where the death of that person has been caused by the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of action,
--       shall be calculated without reference to any loss or gain to the person's estate consequent on the person's
--       death, except that a sum in respect of funeral expenses may be included,
--   (d) where the death of that person has been caused by the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of action,
--       shall not include any damages for the pain or suffering of that person or for any bodily or mental harm suffered
--       by the person or for the curtailment of the person's expectation of life."
-- s 2(7) "This section has effect subject to section 12B of the Dust Diseases Tribunal Act 1989." (s 12B(2) of that Act
-- disapplies s 2(2)(d) for proceedings commenced by the person before death and pending before the Dust Diseases
-- Tribunal at death, where the cause of action is for damages for a dust-related condition. Its terms are asked.)
-- Flat negatives ("shall not include", "calculated without reference", "limited to"): left out. A head s 2(2) does not
-- touch is "not cut down": s 2(2) gives no head of damages itself (fork F4).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of claim`
      `the death was caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN
      `dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN
      `pending before the Tribunal at death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the damages` IS A `Kind of damages`
GIVETH A `What section 2(2) means for these damages`
`what s 2(2) does to` `the kind` `the death was caused by the wrong` `dust-related condition` `pending before the Tribunal at death` `the damages` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the damages` EQUALS `exemplary damages` THEN `left out`
           IF `the damages` EQUALS `loss of earning capacity or earnings for the years after death the person would have lived but for the wrong` THEN `left out`
           IF `the kind` EQUALS `breach of promise to marry`
           THEN IF `the damages` EQUALS `damage to the estate flowing from a breach of promise to marry`
                THEN `not cut down`
                ELSE `left out`
           IF `the damages` EQUALS `damage to the estate flowing from a breach of promise to marry` THEN `this Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (the head is defined only by s 2(2)(b), for a breach of promise to marry; fork F5)
           IF `the damages` EQUALS `pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, or curtailment of expectation of life`
           THEN IF (NOT `the death was caused by the wrong`)
                THEN `not cut down`
                ELSE IF `dust-related condition` AND `pending before the Tribunal at death`
                     THEN `not cut down`
                     ELSE `left out`
           IF `the damages` EQUALS `funeral expenses`
           THEN IF `the death was caused by the wrong`
                THEN `funeral expenses may be included`
                ELSE `this Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: s 2(2)(c) speaks of funeral expenses only where the wrong caused the death; fork F6)
           IF     `the damages` EQUALS `another loss to the estate because of the death`
               OR `the damages` EQUALS `a gain to the estate because of the death, such as insurance paid on death`
           THEN IF `the death was caused by the wrong`
                THEN `left out`
                ELSE `this Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: s 2(2)(c) is a "where" rule for a death the wrong caused; fork F6)
           OTHERWISE `not cut down`
    WHERE
        `left out` MEANS `left out: the estate's damages do not include it, or are worked out without it`
        `not cut down` MEANS `not cut down by section 2(2): the estate recovers it if the cause of action gives it`

@export Can the estate of the person who died recover this kind of damages on the claim that person had (s 2(1), (2), (7))?
GIVEN `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc The kind of cause of action the person who died had. One of: "defamation", "seduction", "inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other", "a claim under Division 2 of Part 3 of the Property (Relationships) Act 1984", "breach of promise to marry", "a claim by relatives under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897", "any other cause of action" (for example negligence causing injury)
      `the death was after the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died after this Act commenced (in 1944); answer yes for any death since then
      `the cause of action existed when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died already had the cause of action at the moment of death
      `the death was caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission the claim is about caused the person's death
      `the claim is for a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for damages in respect of a dust-related condition (as the Dust Diseases Tribunal Act 1989 uses the term, for example mesothelioma or asbestosis)
      `their own proceedings were pending before the Dust Diseases Tribunal when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had started proceedings on the claim in the Dust Diseases Tribunal, and they were still pending at the death
      `the damages` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc The kind of damages you are asking about. One of: "exemplary damages", "loss of earning capacity or earnings for the years after death the person would have lived but for the wrong", "loss of earnings or earning capacity up to the death", "medical, care and other expenses incurred before the death", "pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, or curtailment of expectation of life", "funeral expenses", "another loss to the estate because of the death", "a gain to the estate because of the death, such as insurance paid on death", "damage to the estate flowing from a breach of promise to marry"
GIVETH A `What section 2(2) means for these damages`
`Can the estate recover these damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `Does the claim survive the death` `the person who had the claim, whose estate I act for` `the kind of claim` `the death was after the Act commenced` `the cause of action existed when they died` FALSE FALSE
    WHEN JUST b THEN
        IF b
        THEN `what s 2(2) does to` `the kind of claim` `the death was caused by the wrong` `the claim is for a dust-related condition` `their own proceedings were pending before the Dust Diseases Tribunal when they died` `the damages`
        ELSE `the claim does not survive for the estate, so there are no estate damages under this Act`
    OTHERWISE `this Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (s 2(1) does not reach the claim: forks F1, F2)

§§ `s 2(5), (6) -- other claims are not cut down`

-- s 2(5) "The rights conferred by this Part for the benefit of the estates of deceased persons shall be in addition to and
-- not in derogation of any rights conferred on the dependants of deceased persons by the Compensation to Relatives Act
-- 1897 ...". s 2(6) "The rights conferred by this Part for the benefit of the estates of deceased persons and the
-- obligations continued or created by this Part against the estates of deceased persons shall be in addition to and not
-- in derogation of any rights conferred or obligations created by or under the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance)
-- Act 1942 or the Transport Accidents Compensation Act 1987 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988."
-- "not in derogation of" is a flat negative: JUST FALSE. Another Act is not named: NOTHING.
@export Does the estate's claim under this Part take away or cut down rights under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 or the motor accidents Acts named in s 2(6) (s 2(5), (6))?
GIVEN `the other scheme` IS AN `Other scheme` @desc The other rights you are asking about. One of: "the dependants' claim under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897", "the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance) Act 1942", "the Transport Accidents Compensation Act 1987", "the Motor Accidents Act 1988", "another Act"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the estate's claim cut down rights under that Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the other scheme`
    WHEN `another Act` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: ss 2(5) and 2(6) name only these Acts; finding 4)
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
